NNAAMMEE
     dduu — display disk usage statistics

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     dduu [--aacchhkkrrssxx] [--HH | --LL | --PP] [--dd _d_e_p_t_h] [_f_i_l_e _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The dduu utility displays the file system block usage for each _f_i_l_e argu‐
     ment and for each directory in the file hierarchy rooted in each direc‐
     tory argument.  Note that the system block usage is usually greater than
     the actual size of the file.  If no file is specified, the block usage of
     the hierarchy rooted in the current directory is displayed.

     The options are as follows:

     --aa      Display entries for files in addition to entries for directories.

     --cc      Display the grand total after all the arguments have been pro‐
             cessed.

     --dd _d_e_p_t_h
             Do not display entries for files and directories more than _d_e_p_t_h
             levels deep; --dd 00 has the same effect as --ss.  Overrides earlier
             --dd and --ss options.

     --HH      Symbolic links on the command line are followed.  Symbolic links
             encountered in the tree traversal are not followed.

     --hh      "Human-readable" output.  Use unit suffixes: Byte, Kilobyte,
             Megabyte, Gigabyte, Terabyte, Petabyte, Exabyte in order to re‐
             duce the number of digits to four or less.

     --kk      By default, all sizes are reported in 512-byte block counts.  The
             --kk option causes the numbers to be reported in kilobyte counts.

     --LL      All symbolic links are followed.

     --PP      No symbolic links are followed.

     --rr      Generate messages about directories that cannot be read, files
             that cannot be opened, and so on.  This is the default.

     --ss      Display only the total for each of the specified files and direc‐
             tories.  Overrides earlier --dd options.

     --xx      File system mount points are not traversed.

     It is not an error to specify more than one of the mutually exclusive op‐
     tions --hh and --kk.  Where more than one of these options is specified, the
     last option given overrides the others.

     dduu counts the storage used by symbolic links and not the files they ref‐
     erence unless the --HH or --LL option is specified.  If either the --HH or --LL
     options are specified, storage used by any symbolic links which are fol‐
     lowed is not counted or displayed.  The --HH, --LL, and --PP options override
     each other and the command's actions are determined by the last one spec‐
     ified.

     Files having multiple hard links are counted (and displayed) a single
     time per dduu execution.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     BLOCKSIZE  Block counts will be displayed in units of this size block,
                unless the --kk or --hh option is specified.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The dduu utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Display a summary of files and folders in the current directory, sorted
     by size:

           $ du -had 1 | sort -h

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     df(1), fts_open(3), symlink(7), quot(8)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The dduu utility is compliant with the IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”)
     specification.

     The flags [--ccddhhPP], as well as the BLOCKSIZE environment variable, are ex‐
     tensions to that specification.

     The flag [--rr] is accepted but ignored, for compatibility with systems im‐
     plementing the obsolete X/Open Commands and Utilities Issue 5 (“XCU5”)
     standard.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The dduu utility first appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX.

